        "thinking": "Now I have a clear picture. To run this locally with `wrangler dev`, I need:\n\n1. A local PostgreSQL database (the `.env.example` shows `postgres://paperclip:paperclip@localhost:5432/paperclip`)\n2. A `.dev.vars` file with the secrets\n3. Update `wrangler.toml` to add a `localConnectionString` for the Hyperdrive binding for local dev\n\nFor `wrangler dev`, the key configuration is:\n- The Hyperdrive binding needs a `localConnectionString` for local testing\n\n\n- KV, R2, Durable Objects, Queues, and Workflows will all be simulated locally\n\nI should verify if there's already a local database running and check the current environment setup. Looking at the `wrangler.toml`, the Hyperdrive binding currently just has the binding name and ID, so I can add the `localConnectionString` parameter pointing to the local PostgreSQL instance for development.\n\nFor the KV binding with `FILL_IN_AFTER_DEPLOY`, the ID doesn't really matter in local mode since wrangler uses a local SQLite store instead. The `preview_id` is what matters for remote preview environments, but `--local` mode is more flexible. Let me check if there's a local Postgres instance running first, then try running `wrangler dev` from the deploy-cloudflare package to see what happens.",
